Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether the matrix sum $\sum_{g \in G} \pi_1(g)\,\pi_2(g^{-1})^\top$ vanishes when $\pi_1 \neq \pi_2$ are distinct irreducible representations of the same degree $d$.

**Answer: Yes, the matrix sum is the zero matrix.**

**Proof.**

For finite groups, every representation is equivalent to a unitary one, so we may assume $\pi_1, \pi_2$ are unitary. The **Schur orthogonality relations** for matrix coefficients of irreducible unitary representations state:

$$\sum_{g \in G} \pi_i(g)_{ab}\,\overline{\pi_j(g)_{cd}} = \frac{|G|}{d}\,\delta_{ij}\,\delta_{ad}\,\delta_{bc}$$

Since $\pi_j$ is unitary, $\pi_j(g^{-1}) = \pi_j(g)^\dagger = \overline{\pi_j(g)}^\top$, so $\pi_j(g^{-1})_{cd} = \overline{\pi_j(g)_{dc}}$. Substituting (with index relabeling):

$$\sum_{g \in G} \pi_i(g)_{ab}\,\pi_j(g^{-1})_{cd} = \frac{|G|}{d}\,\delta_{ij}\,\delta_{ac}\,\delta_{bd}$$

Now let $S = \sum_{g \in G} \pi_1(g)\,\pi_2(g^{-1})^\top$. Its $(a,c)$-entry is:

$$S_{ac} = \sum_{g \in G}\sum_{b=1}^{d} \pi_1(g)_{ab}\,\bigl[\pi_2(g^{-1})^\top\bigr]_{bc} = \sum_{b=1}^{d}\sum_{g \in G} \pi_1(g)_{ab}\,\pi_2(g^{-1})_{cb}$$

Applying the orthogonality relation with $i=1$, $j=2$, where the indices of $\pi_1(g)$ are $(a,b)$ and those of $\pi_2(g^{-1})$ are $(c,b)$ (mapping $c \mapsto c$, $b \mapsto d$ in the formula):

$$\sum_{g \in G} \pi_1(g)_{ab}\,\pi_2(g^{-1})_{cb} = \frac{|G|}{d}\,\delta_{12}\,\delta_{ac}\,\underbrace{\delta_{bb}}_{=\,1} = \frac{|G|}{d}\,\delta_{12}\,\delta_{ac}$$

Summing over $b = 1, \dots, d$:

$$S_{ac} = \sum_{b=1}^{d} \frac{|G|}{d}\,\delta_{12}\,\delta_{ac} = |G|\,\delta_{12}\,\delta_{ac}$$

Since $\pi_1 \neq \pi_2$ (non-isomorphic), $\delta_{12} = 0$, giving $S_{ac} = 0$ for all $a, c$.

$$\boxed{S = \sum_{g \in G} \pi_1(g)\,\pi_2(g^{-1})^\top = \mathbf{0}}$$

**Remark.** The given character orthogonality $\sum_g \chi_1(g)\,\chi_2(g)^* = 0$ is the *trace* of the matrix-coefficient orthogonality (set $a{=}b$, $c{=}d$, sum over $a,c$). The vanishing of $S$ is the stronger, full matrix-level consequence of the same Schur orthogonality relations: character orthogonality uses only the "diagonal" matrix coefficients, while $S = 0$ requires all of them simultaneously.

